
[Fixed] Searching trappist or trappist1 finds TRAPPIST-1, and planet suffixes remain distinct.

[Fixed in better-atmospheres branch] Pressure haze affects clouds, rocks, and plants. Clouds use their viewing distance, and scenery attenuation uses physical range independently of camera sorting depth.

[Fixed] Sun-clock star and planet/moon icons have black outlines, and non-star icons have offset shadows for contrast against the surface texture.

[Fixed] Sun-clock illumination uses one-quarter stellar color saturation in both CPU and GPU rendering.

[Fixed in better-atmospheres branch] Plant crowns and trunks are clipped independently. Visible trunk segments remain when the crown passes behind the camera, with finite near-plane clipping and matching CPU/GPU rendering.

Companion stars brighter than magnitude -10 should appear in sun clock mode even if the current object orbits the main star.

Companion stars, nearby planets, and bright moons should cast shadows in horizon mode proportional in strength and color to the casting object's brightness and hue relative to the light center.

[Fixed in better-atmospheres branch] Rocks use varied faceted profiles and deterministic aspect ratios. A continuous, bounded logarithmic pressure bias favors flatter shapes in dense atmospheres, replacing the Venus-specific slab switch. This is a visual approximation rather than a geological law.
